    "media:imagetext": "PAA\nPAN AMERICAN AIRWAYS COMPANY\nGENERAL OFFICE\nALAMEDA - CALIFORNIA\nDISTRICT TRAFFIC OFFICE\nMANILA HOTEL\nMANILA, P. I.\nBeguio, P.I.\nOot. 11, 1937\nLetter No. B-23-37.\nPan American Airways Company\nNew York.\nAttention Mr. Morgan:\nP A N\nDear Mr. Morgan:\nI was very much interested in your letter of September 22nd and\nyour analysis of the developments which led up to the opening of the Sino.\nJapanese hostilities would seem to indicate that a student of Chinese af-\nfairs far removed from the daily gossip and rumors of the street is probab-\nly in a better position to judge the value of the forces than one who is,\nas you state, so close to the trees that he can't see the woods.\nI do not entirely agree with you that China\n\"came out from under the desk\nwith a brass knuckle on either hand\".\nIt is true that China has been pre-\nparing but that preparation was no where near complete as evidenced by\nfact that China ia already running short of many essential materials.\ngo on to say that there is considerable talk in New York and elsewhere to\nthe effect that the Chinese Government was dragged into the war through\npublie opinion and inability to restrain the military leaders and masses\nof the people any longer and then you go on to state that in your opinion\nthis theory is unsound and point in support of your position to the ample\npreparations made against a war with Japan.\nI am personally still of the opinion that Chiang Kai-shek wes\nand desired to go to any ends rather than permit the starting\nof hostilities which might lead to his own overthrow and a complete de•\nfeat of China.\nI believe he would have gladly worked out some compromise\nfor \u0026 show of war in the North to be followed by definite concessions in-\ncluding an autonomous state in the Province of Hopei and probably north\nShantung•\nMost authorities agree that the actual starting of hostilities\nin the Shanghai area was brought on by the Chinese rather than the Japanese\nas the latter had been unusually conciliatory with respect to all incidents\nin the Shanghai area.\nIn this connection I had a very interesting talk\nwith a man here who is known to you as an outstanding military strategist.\nHe states that only the results justify the action of the Japanese in de-\nfending\ntheir position at Shanghai. It was known that there were only\n10,000 Japanese troops in the Shanghai area as against not less than 60,000\nChinese,\nincluding the best troops that General Chiang could put in the\nfield.\nI told this party that a Chinese official informed me on the night\nof August 14th that the Japanese would be pushed into the Whangpoo that\nnight and the same party repeated that statement on the 15th.\nOur mutual\nfriend here stated that there wes no excuse whatever for the Chinese not\ncompleting that objective and that if he had been in the position of the\nJapanese Commander he would have taken to the boats and left Shanghai\n-1 3 !",
